+<p>If you die before about 82, you get back less (in present value) than
+the 6.710094^{4} you paid; if you live longer, you get back more. Dying
+at 75 returns 4.5517^{4}, a loss, and living to 95 returns 9.8389^{4}, a
+gain. The premium is the expected value of the benefits, so averaged
+over everyone who buys it the product is a fair deal (before any insurer
+profit or expenses). Whether it is a good deal for <em>you</em> depends
+on whether you expect to live longer than average.</p>
