+<p><strong>Using GLMs to Model Mortality: the Gompertz Law</strong></p>
+<p>Adult mortality follows a well-known pattern called the Gompertz law:
+the death rate grows exponentially with age, so log(mx) is close to a
+straight line in age. That makes it a natural GLM: a Poisson model for
+the deaths dx, with a log link, Age as the predictor and log(Lx)
+(person-years lived) as an offset, so that the fitted quantity is the
+death rate mx = dx / Lx.</p>
+<p>We fit ages 30-100 of the 2021 table. Below 30, mortality has an
+infant and young-adult accident “hump” that a straight line can’t
+capture. Above 100, the HMD values are themselves smoothed by a model
+rather than observed directly.</p>
+<p>One caveat applies to every model in this section: a life table’s dx
+and Lx are scaled to a hypothetical 100,000 births, not real counts. The
+fitted rates are valid, but the standard errors, p-values and AIC values
+describe that hypothetical population, so read them as relative rather
+than absolute. Real counts are in the HMD <code>Deaths_1x1</code> and
+<code>Exposures_1x1</code> files.</p>
+<div class="sourceCode" id="cb199"><pre
+class="sourceCode r"><code class="sourceCode r"><span id="cb199-1"><a href="#cb199-1" tabindex="-1"></a>adults_2021 <span class="ot">&lt;-</span> <span class="fu">subset</span>(life_table_2021, Age <span class="sc">&gt;=</span> <span class="dv">30</span> <span class="sc">&amp;</span> Age <span class="sc">&lt;=</span> <span class="dv">100</span>)</span>
+<span id="cb199-2"><a href="#cb199-2" tabindex="-1"></a>gompertz <span class="ot">&lt;-</span> <span class="fu">glm</span>(dx <span class="sc">~</span> Age <span class="sc">+</span> <span class="fu">offset</span>(<span class="fu">log</span>(Lx)), <span class="at">family =</span> poisson, <span class="at">data =</span> adults_2021)</span>
+<span id="cb199-3"><a href="#cb199-3" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="fu">coef</span>(gompertz)</span></code></pre></div>
+<pre><code>##  (Intercept)          Age 
+## -11.03696448   0.09916235</code></pre>
