+<p><img src="figures/unnamed-chunk-81-1.png" width="672" /></p>
+<p>From about age 55 upward the fitted line tracks the data closely:
+mortality roughly doubles every 7 years of age, the classic Gompertz
+result. Between 30 and 55 the actual rates sit above the line, because
+deaths at those ages include more accidents, overdoses and other causes
+that don’t rise exponentially with age. The Poisson fit is driven by
+where most deaths happen (older ages), so it follows those ages most
+closely.</p>
+<p><em>Why the earlier version of this chart looked wrong:</em> it
+plotted all 101 years (1921-2021) in one <code>lines()</code> call. Each
+time the line reached age 110 for one year, it jumped back to age 0 for
+the next year, and those jumps were the diagonal bands. It also fitted
+qx as a logistic curve that is straight in age, which can’t represent
+mortality that rises exponentially with age and also has an infant
+spike. That’s why the red curve missed badly.</p>
+<p><strong>Adding calendar year: mortality improvement</strong></p>
+<p>Adding Year to the model measures how fast mortality has fallen over
+time at a given age.</p>
+<div class="sourceCode" id="cb204"><pre
+class="sourceCode r"><code class="sourceCode r"><span id="cb204-1"><a href="#cb204-1" tabindex="-1"></a>adults_all <span class="ot">&lt;-</span> <span class="fu">subset</span>(life_table_all, Age <span class="sc">&gt;=</span> <span class="dv">30</span> <span class="sc">&amp;</span> Age <span class="sc">&lt;=</span> <span class="dv">100</span>)</span>
+<span id="cb204-2"><a href="#cb204-2" tabindex="-1"></a>gompertz_year <span class="ot">&lt;-</span> <span class="fu">glm</span>(dx <span class="sc">~</span> Age <span class="sc">+</span> Year <span class="sc">+</span> <span class="fu">offset</span>(<span class="fu">log</span>(Lx)), <span class="at">family =</span> poisson, <span class="at">data =</span> adults_all)</span>
+<span id="cb204-3"><a href="#cb204-3" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="fu">coef</span>(gompertz_year)</span></code></pre></div>
