+<p><img src="figures/unnamed-chunk-82-1.png" width="672" /></p>
+<p>On average, adult mortality fell by about 1.1% a year. The model
+assumes the same improvement at every age and in every year, which is
+too simple, and the chart shows where it breaks down. In 1921 the actual
+rates sit above the line at younger ages and below it at older ages,
+meaning younger adults have since improved faster than average and the
+oldest more slowly. In 2021 the actual rates sit above the line at most
+ages, because improvement has slowed in recent years and 2021 was a
+pandemic year. The GAM below relaxes these assumptions.</p>
+<p><strong>A flexible model: a GAM over age and year</strong></p>
+<p>A generalized additive model (GAM) replaces the straight lines with a
+smooth surface over age and year, so improvement can differ by age. It
+uses the same Poisson setup, now over ages 0-100. An earlier version
+also used mx as a predictor; that is circular, because qx is calculated
+from mx, so the model was just recovering its own input.</p>
