 <div id="arimaarma-models-for-mortality-rate-forecasting"
 class="section level2">
 <h2>ARIMA/ARMA Models for Mortality Rate Forecasting</h2>
-<p><strong>We will use ARIMA and ARMA models to forecast future
-mortality rates.</strong></p>
+<p><strong>We will use an ARIMA model to forecast life expectancy at
+birth.</strong></p>
+<p>An earlier version averaged qx over all ages 0-110 each year and
+forecast that. That average isn’t a meaningful mortality measure: it
+gives the same weight to age 109 as to age 30, so it is dominated by the
+very old ages where qx is 0.3 to 1. Life expectancy at birth summarises
+the whole year’s mortality in one number that people understand, so we
+forecast that instead.</p>
 <details>
 <summary>
 <span style="color:blue;">Explanation of the Code and Graph</span>
 </summary>
 <p>
-### Explanation of the Code
-</p>
-<p>
-#### What We Are Doing in the Code
-</p>
-<p>
 <ol style="list-style-type: decimal">
-<li><strong>Aggregating Mortality Rates</strong>:
+<li><strong>Series:</strong> life expectancy at birth (e0) for each year
+1921-2021, stored as an annual time series.
 </p>
-<ul>
-<li>
-We are averaging mortality rates (<code>qx</code>) by year from a
-dataset called <code>life_table</code>.
-</li>
-<li>
-This helps us get a yearly average mortality rate.
-</li>
-</ul></li>
-</ol>
-<pre><code>agg_mortality <- aggregate(qx ~ Year, data = life_table, FUN = mean)</code></pre>
 <p>
 <ol start="2" style="list-style-type: decimal">
-<li><strong>Fitting an ARIMA Model</strong>:
+<li><strong>Model:</strong> <code>auto.arima()</code> chooses the ARIMA
+orders by AIC. Life expectancy trends upward rather than fluctuating
+around a fixed level, so the model works with differences of the series
+(the “I” in ARIMA) instead of the raw values.
 </p>
-<ul>
-<li>
-We use an ARIMA (AutoRegressive Integrated Moving Average) model to fit
-the historical mortality rates.
-</li>
-<li>
-The ARIMA model helps us identify patterns in the data and forecast
-future values.
-</li>
-</ul></li>
-</ol>
-<pre><code>qx_ts <- ts(agg_mortality$qx, start = min(agg_mortality$Year))
-arima_model <- auto.arima(qx_ts)
-summary(arima_model)</code></pre>
 <p>
 <ol start="3" style="list-style-type: decimal">
-<li><strong>Forecasting Future Mortality Rates</strong>:
+<li><strong>Forecast:</strong> 10 years ahead, with 80% and 95%
+prediction intervals shown as shaded bands.
 </p>
-<ul>
-<li>
-Using the ARIMA model, we forecast future mortality rates for the next
-10 years.
-</li>
-</ul></li>
-</ol>
-<pre><code>forecasts_arima <- forecast(arima_model, h = 10)</code></pre>
-<p>
-<ol start="4" style="list-style-type: decimal">
-<li><strong>Plotting the Data</strong>:
-</p>
-<ul>
-<li>
-We plot both the historical mortality rates and the forecasted future
-rates.
-</li>
-<li>
-This helps visualize trends over time and the predicted future values.
-</li>
-</ul></li>
+</details></li>
+</ol></li>
+</ol></li>
 </ol>
